---
import DocumentationLayout from '../layouts/DocumentationLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import TryInClaudeCode from '../components/sections/TryInClaudeCode.astro';
import { buildWebPageJsonLD } from '../utils/seo';
import installTruth from '../data/install_truth.json';
import heroGetStarted from '../assets/heroes/hero_get_started.png';

// Every command on this page renders from the install-truth fixture (single source;
// gate-12-install-truth asserts the page matches it and the fixture matches the
// template checkout). Never hardcode an install command here. (ADR-034: canonical
// face = aDNA-Network/aDNA, a clone-and-run workspace image; 1-command flow.)
const { commands, workspace_root, canonical_repo_https, legacy_repo_https } = installTruth;

const jsonLD = buildWebPageJsonLD({
  title: 'Get Started with aDNA',
  description:
    'Set up your own local aDNA workspace in one command: clone the workspace image from GitHub — router pre-instantiated, standard embedded — and let your first agent session scaffold your first project.',
  url: Astro.url.href,
});
---

<DocumentationLayout
  title="Get Started"
  description="Set up your own local aDNA workspace in one command: clone the workspace image from GitHub — router pre-instantiated, standard embedded — and let your first agent session scaffold your first project."
  jsonLD={jsonLD}
  heroImage={{
    src: heroGetStarted,
    alt: 'Cozy pixel-art scene of a desk and computer with a glowing cyan DNA helix rising from the screen — a new aDNA workspace coming alive',
    /* HAUSSMANN P4.1 O2 — the first exercise of ADR-053's `hero_panel` credit law, which the ADR
       records as NORMATIVE and UNMET. Added here rather than nowhere so the field does not ship as
       an authored-but-unexercised instrument.

       ⭐ EVERY FACT IN THIS LINE WAS VERIFIED AT THE OBJECT, not quoted from a status line. The
       model, register and cycle come from the generation ledger itself —
       what/measurement/iii_results/2026-06/cycle_157_e1_hero_helix.r1.image_gen_log.json — which
       records `"model": "imagen-4.0-ultra-generate-001"` and `"register": "ss_ghibli_pixel /
       tokyo_night"`. This asset was promoted from that run's candidate `helix_r1_H2_v2`.

       ⛔ SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISREAD AS DONE: this is ONE of the ten live `hero_panel`
       routes. The other nine are still uncredited, and ADR-053's credit law therefore remains
       UNMET for the hero slot. AC5 covers the ONE NEW slot (`empty_state`), which is fully
       credited; the hero backlog is named in the mission record, not implied away by this line. */
    credit:
      'Illustration generated with Google Imagen 4 Ultra (imagen-4.0-ultra-generate-001) in the ss-ghibli-pixel / Tokyo Night register, commissioned under ADR-032 and now governed by ADR-053.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    Set up your own aDNA workspace — a local, agent-readable home for every project's context — in
    about 5 minutes. Everything stays on your machine. Nothing is sent anywhere.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="Prerequisites">
    <p>
      Git, and <a href="https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code</a>
      installed via <code>npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code</code>. Obsidian is optional — vaults are
      plain Markdown, so any editor works.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout variant="tip" title="What you'll have when you're done">
    <p>
      A <code>{workspace_root}/</code> workspace that ships ready: the workspace <code>CLAUDE.md</code> router
      (your agent's map between projects) pre-instantiated at the root; the standard itself embedded in a hidden
      <code>.adna/</code> folder (your agent reads it, never edits it — updates arrive with <code>git pull</code>
      at the workspace root); and your first <code>&lt;name&gt;.aDNA/</code> project, scaffolded for you when the
      agent follows the standard's project-fork skill, with its own git history — kept out of the workspace
      image's history by design.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  {/* HAUSSMANN P2.5 O1 — the cost statement (design §5, shipped as drafted at the ⛩ pick).
      Placed ABOVE the first command rather than beside the one-liner at the foot of the page:
      the objection it answers is one a reader forms before running anything, so answering it
      after they have already been shown the command is answering it too late. */}
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What this command does, before you run it</h2>
  <p class="cmd-oneliner"><code>{installTruth.one_liner}</code></p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Writes one directory: <code>{workspace_root}/</code>.</strong> Nothing outside it — no system
      settings, no <code>PATH</code> changes, no daemon, no login item. If you would rather not use that path,
      clone anywhere: nothing in the workspace depends on its location, and <code>{workspace_root}</code> is
      only the default the docs assume.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The <code>&amp;&amp; claude</code> starts an agent in that directory</strong>, which reads the
      instruction files you just cloned — <code>CLAUDE.md</code> at the root, and the standard in
      <code>.adna/</code>. That is the real trust question here, and it is a fair one: these files are
      prompt-ware, and prompt-ware is executed by the agent that reads it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>So read them first.</strong>
      <a href="/get-started/what-your-agent-reads/">Every file the agent reads on first run, annotated →</a>
      They are shown verbatim, at a pinned commit, with hashes you can check against your own clone.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Nothing is sent anywhere.</strong> No account, no telemetry, no network call after the clone.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>This is a Claude Code convention.</strong> The workspace is plain Markdown that any tool can read,
      but this one-command flow assumes Claude Code specifically. Other agents can read the same files; they
      will not run this command.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>To undo it: <code>rm -rf {workspace_root}</code>.</strong> There is nothing else to uninstall.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="step-1">1. Clone the workspace</h2>
  <p>
    The clone <em>is</em> your workspace — not a template you copy from. The router comes pre-instantiated
    and the standard comes embedded; there is nothing to bootstrap by hand.
  </p>
  <pre class="cmd"><code>{commands.clone}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="step-2">2. Start Claude Code</h2>
  <p>
    Run <code>{commands.launch}</code> in the workspace. The agent reads the router, sees a fresh workspace
    with no projects yet, and walks you through creating your first one:
  </p>
  <pre class="cmd"><code>{commands.enter}
{commands.launch}</code></pre>

  {/* HAUSSMANN P2.5 O1 — R-118. A rendered terminal block used to sit here, depicting output that
      exists nowhere in the standard (searched .adna/ in full: zero hits) and dramatising an
      onboarding interview that does not fire on a fresh clone. It was cut at the ⛩ pick rather
      than refilled: the honest replacement is a real recorded session, and that arrives from the
      clean-machine run at P2.5 O2. The gap is LABELLED because P2.4's lesson is that a
      considerate-looking absence is where a defect hides. Do not fill this with an illustration. */}
  <Callout variant="info" title="What a first run looks like — not yet recorded">
    <p>
      There was a sample transcript here. We wrote it by hand, and it showed output the software does
      not actually print, so we removed it rather than leave a plausible-looking invention on the page
      you use to decide whether to trust us. A real recording, from a clean machine, replaces it once
      we have made that run and measured it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the meantime the honest version of the same thing is the source:
      <a href="/get-started/what-your-agent-reads/">read the files your agent reads</a>, including the
      project-fork skill it follows here — step by step, so you can predict what appears on disk.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    With no project in the workspace yet, the router sends the agent to the standard's
    project-fork skill, which scaffolds <code>&lt;your_project&gt;.aDNA/</code> — its triad of
    <code>what/</code>&thinsp;·&thinsp;<code>how/</code>&thinsp;·&thinsp;<code>who/</code>, its governance
    files, and its own git history. It then offers to run an onboarding interview that customises the
    new project for your domain; you can take it now or leave it for the first session inside the
    project. When the scaffolding finishes, your workspace looks like this:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{workspace_root}/
├── CLAUDE.md            # workspace router (ships with the clone)
├── .adna/               # the standard, embedded (read-only)
└── my_project.aDNA/     # your first project — a context graph
    ├── CLAUDE.md        # the project's own governance
    ├── what/            # knowledge: context, decisions, artifacts
    ├── how/             # operations: sessions, missions, skills
    └── who/             # people: roles, coordination</code></pre>
  <p>
    From here, work happens <em>inside</em> projects: <code>cd my_project.aDNA &amp;&amp; {commands.launch}</code>
    and the agent picks up that project's governance automatically.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="The whole flow, one command">
    <p>
      Both steps, chained — auditable inline, nothing executed from the network:
    </p>
    <p class="cmd-oneliner"><code>{installTruth.one_liner}</code></p>
  </Callout>

  {/* HAUSSMANN P2.5 O1 — "first success", defined observably (design §2, accepted at the ⛩ pick
      as the stop-condition for the O2 clean-machine run). Both halves ship: a success definition
      the reader cannot check is an adjective. NOTE: no timing claim is authored here — R-34/R-63
      are discharged by O2's measurement or revised down then, never by copy. */}
  <h2 id="check-it-worked">3. Check it worked</h2>
  <p>
    "It worked" should be something you can verify, not something you feel. Five commands — each
    prints nothing and exits <code>0</code> on success, except the last two, which print your project's
    contents and its first commit:
  </p>
  <pre class="cmd"><code>test -f {workspace_root}/CLAUDE.md            # the router your agent reads first
test -d {workspace_root}/.adna                # the standard, embedded and read-only
ls -d {workspace_root}/*.aDNA                 # at least one project vault exists
ls {workspace_root}/&lt;name&gt;.aDNA/what {workspace_root}/&lt;name&gt;.aDNA/how {workspace_root}/&lt;name&gt;.aDNA/who
git -C {workspace_root}/&lt;name&gt;.aDNA log      # its own history, separate from the image's</code></pre>
  <p>
    Replace <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> with whatever you called your project. That the last one has its own
    git log — not the workspace image's — is the check people most often skip and the one that matters:
    your context graph is your repository from the first commit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Then the half that is actually the point.</strong> Open a <em>new</em> agent session inside
    <code>&lt;name&gt;.aDNA/</code> and ask it something about the project. It should already know where it
    is and what the project's governance says, without you telling it where to look. The five commands
    prove files were written; this proves the thing the standard exists for.
  </p>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">If something goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    The surface here is small — a clone and an agent launch — so the list of ways it fails is short.
    These are the failures the two commands can actually produce:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>{commands.launch}: command not found</code> — Claude Code is not installed, or not on your
      <code>PATH</code>. Install it with <code>npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code</code> and open a
      new shell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>fatal: destination path '{workspace_root}' already exists</code> — you already have a workspace
      there. Either work in it, or clone somewhere else; nothing depends on the path.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The agent does not seem to know about aDNA.</strong> It is almost always started in the
      wrong directory. The router is <code>CLAUDE.md</code> at the workspace root, so
      <code>{commands.enter}</code> first — an agent launched from your home directory reads nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You want to check what the agent is acting on.</strong> Every file it reads on first run is
      <a href="/get-started/what-your-agent-reads/">published here verbatim</a>, with hashes to compare
      against your clone.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This list is short because it is drawn from what the commands can do, not from a support inbox.
    It will grow as real installs are recorded.
  </p>

  <h2 id="removing-it">Removing it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>rm -rf {workspace_root}</code> — and that is the whole uninstall. Nothing was installed outside
    that directory, so there is no package to remove, no configuration to revert, and no service to stop.
    If you moved your projects elsewhere first, they are ordinary git repositories and keep working.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="info" title="Existing installs (pre-June-2026 layout)">
    <p>
      Installed earlier, with a cloned hidden <code>.adna/</code> plus a copied router? It keeps working:
      the previous template repository is preserved read-only at
      <a href={legacy_repo_https} target="_blank" rel="noopener">adna-legacy</a>, and your clone's old URLs
      redirect there — frozen, so nothing underneath you changes. To move to the current layout, run the
      standard's <code>skill_workspace_upgrade</code> from your workspace.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <TryInClaudeCode />

  <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
  {/* Storyweave P3 / M3.2 — the north-star bridge: make the loop explicit (you built a node → it
      joins a living network), not just a list of "learn more" links. */}
  <p>You just built a node in a living network. Grow it — then see where it connects:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Where it joins:</strong> the <a href="/vaults/">living registry</a> of vaults and <a href="/network/">the network</a> your project can federate with — building in the open is the whole point.</li>
    <li><strong>Ready to shape it:</strong> aDNA is built in the open — see <a href="/community/community-contribution-standards/">how to propose a change</a> to the standard or contribute a pattern, vault, or idea.</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/what-is-adna">What is aDNA?</a> — the five-minute conceptual tour</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/concepts/triad">The Triad</a> — why every project splits into what / how / who</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/tutorials/first-claude-md">Tutorial: Create Your First CLAUDE.md</a></li>
    <li><a href="/learn/tutorials/navigate-a-vault">Tutorial: Navigate a Vault</a> — reading a context graph like an agent does</li>
    <li><a href="/learn/concepts/knowledge-graph">Knowledge graphs</a> — what your workspace grows into</li>
    <li>See it live: the <a href={canonical_repo_https} target="_blank" rel="noopener">aDNA repository</a> is the workspace image itself — clone it and you are holding everything this page described.</li>
  </ul>
</DocumentationLayout>

<style>
  /* H-8: copy-critical install commands must be fully readable at 320–390px — soft-wrap them
     (the long clone URL + the one-liner) rather than hide them behind horizontal scroll. Scoped
     to the command blocks only; the illustrative ASCII tree + interview output keep the default
     overflow-x:auto so their alignment is preserved. */
  pre.cmd {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .cmd-oneliner code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    word-break: break-word;
  }
</style>
